                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi Σ
                            %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Sigma_n // \Sigma_n
                  \&amp;\to&amp;
                  *
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  (\mathbf{B} \Sigma_n)^I &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}\Sigma_n
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\Sigma_n
            is one-object groupoid with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_n
            as its morphisms,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}\Sigma_n)^I
            is its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
            and the bottom vertical map is evaluation at the source. Since this is an acyclic fibration, so is the top vertical morphism.
          %p It follows that the canonical morphism of simplicial operads
          .maruku-equation
